+
+For calibration: the 23 passing question-tasks of the same classes have 0 rival answers and 0 conflicts (rival department
+outside the question's two departments → not routable); the 10 goal-only cross_domain tasks pass as before. No abstain-class
+task (coincidence, copies, single_domain, denied, cross_tenant) has a conflict on its question, and no contradiction/temporal
+task opened one (the retracted/superseded side is never active), so the conflict path is exercised **only** by the 17 failures.
+
+## 3. Why the baseline succeeds on the same tasks
+
+`research/mycelic_e2e/bench/baseline_central.py:_answer_task` runs the same `evaluate_responses` over the same records (dev-025:
+2 findings, 15 disagreements) but commits findings with `has_open_conflict=False` and only the disagreement *sides* with
+`has_open_conflict=True`. Its view therefore holds the gold finding as `supported` (3 roots, 2 departments), the rival finding
+as `hypothesis` (department rule) and 2×15 single-root `contested` side claims. Extraction sees one unambiguous supported claim
+→ correct. It is not a better judgement, it is the absence of the system's "a disagreement demotes both claims" step; the
+system's own gate ranks the two findings exactly the same way before the conflict overwrites it.
+
+## 4. Highest-leverage production changes (ranked)
+
+### C1 — A disagreement between claims about *different* entities is competing evidence, not a contradiction (largest gain)
+
+* **Mechanism today**: `mycelic/discovery/engine.py:commit` (block after `existing_side`/`side_claim`, ~745–780) opens a
+  conflict for every `disagreements[]` pair the evaluator returns; `mycelic/models/fake.py:evaluate_responses` (and
+  `engine._agrees`) call two statements a disagreement when they share ≥3 content tokens and their numbers differ — with
+  `service` counted as a content token, any two "<x>-service … <context phrase>, <n>" records qualify.
+  `knowledge/service.py:_open_conflict_sync` then sets both claims `contested` whatever the gate decided.
+* **One-factor change (engine, model-independent)**: before `open_conflict`, compute the entity sets of the two sides with the
+  existing `engine._claim_entities(..., text)` (hypergraph support context + `hg.entities_in_text`). If **both sides name
+  exactly one entity and the entities differ**, do not open a conflict: commit both sides as ordinary findings (they already
+  are, via `existing_side`) and let the gate's independence rules rank them (`supported` vs `hypothesis`). Record the pair as
+  `competing` in the gate notes / derivation so the audit still explains it. Keep today's behaviour when either side names
+  zero or several entities (a true record contradiction about one thing, or an ambiguous statement). Apply the same guard
+  to the verification path (`engine.py` ~818, `_agrees` on verification findings).
+* **Expected movement**: +17 at S1 (103 → 120/120): in all 17 the gate already says gold=supported, rival=hypothesis.
+  Run-wide, 1241/1690 (73%) conflict investigations disappear, which also returns loop budget to real gaps. Scales: the
+  holdout structure is identical (C9 "identical in dev and holdout"); R does not enter.
+* **Must not break**: contradiction class (same entity, different numbers → conflict must still open; today no contradiction
+  task opens one because the retracted side is inactive, so add a unit test: two claims naming the same service with
+  different numbers → `conflict.open`; different services → no conflict, both committed). Goal-only tasks: 6 passing
+  cross_domain goal-only tasks currently carry `contested` claims — with C1 some become `supported`; re-check that the
+  "most independent roots" rule still picks the gold option (D2 decoy ambiguity). Denied/cross-tenant/copies: untouched (the
+  change is after the gate). Keep `status=contested` for `contradicting_refs` (retractions) as is.
+
+### C2 — Prefer the cross-unit corroborated claim when a conflict *is* opened (gate-rank asymmetry; complement or fallback to C1)
+
+* **Mechanism today**: `_open_conflict_sync` demotes both sides symmetrically; the investigation (`engine.py` ~660–690) can
+  only resolve when `not still and sa and not sb` and `_decisive` finds a *new* root — with the child routed away from both
+  sides (`routing.py:rank_holders`, `W_SUPPORTS=2` applied to the supporters of both conflict claims via `question_entities`
+  → `support.holders`), `sa`/`sb` are 0 and the conflict is never decided.
+* **One-factor change** (`knowledge/service.py:_open_conflict_sync` + `recompute_status`): a side that failed the question's
+  independence policy (gate `hypothesis`: 1 department, or < min roots) cannot demote a side that passed it. The passing
+  side keeps `supported` with the conflict attached (`support.open_conflicts`), the failing side becomes `contested`; both
+  sides demote only when both passed the policy. Pair with the contradiction follow-up actually reaching the holders behind
+  each side as its docstring promises (`_ask_contradiction`: pass `exclude_supporters=False` semantics into routing — zero the
+  `already_supports` penalty for `kind == "contradiction"`), so a real same-entity conflict can still be decided by `_decisive`.
+* **Expected movement**: +17 at S1 on its own (same 17 tasks); with C1 in place it changes nothing at S1 but is the general
+  rule for a real deployment where a single team's loud claim meets a quietly corroborated one.
+* **Must not break**: a genuine same-entity contradiction where the *retraction* lives in one department and the stale value
+  is corroborated across two: the stale side would stay `supported` until the investigation runs. Acceptable only together with
+  the routing part (the investigation then reaches the retracting holder) and `contradicting_refs` still forcing `contested`.
+  Audit: G7 cross-checks claim statuses against `coord.db` — update its expectation for the asymmetric status.
+
+### C3 — Department-diverse routing within budget: round-robin across departments instead of one diversity slot (robustness at R=12 / M / L)
+
+* **Mechanism today**: `routing.py:rank_holders` gives `W_DIVERSITY=1` once per department (first holder), then fills the
+  remaining slots by `base` with a `holder_id` tie-break. In the rival department every rival holder has the same `term_hits`
+  as the gold holders (same context phrase), so beyond the first slot per department the order is arbitrary (dev-017: the
+  gold unit holder lost to user holders with equal scores). At R=12 (holdout range {2,4,8,12}) one department's rivals can
+  take 8 of the 10 slots; the second gold holder of the other department is then a coin flip.
+* **One-factor change**: make the diversity bonus decay with the number already chosen from the group
+  (`W_DIVERSITY / (1 + n_chosen_in_group)`), i.e. each department gets its k-th slot only after every other candidate
+  department got its (k−1)-th; keep `base` ordering inside a department and prefer unit (department memory) holders on exact
+  ties (`owner_type == "unit"` before `holder_id`). Write `n_chosen_in_group` into the audit `scores`.
+* **Expected movement**: 0 at S1 (dev-017 still passed the gate with 2/3); it removes the only routing exposure the rival
+  structure has left, and it is what a deployment with hundreds of holders per department needs anyway.
+* **Must not break**: single_domain (one department → still one department after re-ordering), denied/cross-tenant (the
+  authorized set is unchanged; only its order changes), G4 replay (deterministic; add the tie rule to the audit detail).
+
+### Not recommended
+
+* Changing `fake.py` (`shared_tokens`, dropping `service`): it changes the provider sha and does not help the real provider;
+  the LLM's `disagreements[]` need the same entity guard in the engine.
+* Raising `budget.holders`: the failure is not routing.
+
+## 5. Verification after the change
+
+1. Re-run C4-S1 system; expect cross_domain 40/40, fault 10/10, all other classes unchanged; `conflict.open` count must drop
+   from 1690 to the same-entity pairs only (check with the run-wide query in `summ_c4.py`'s companion: conflicts whose two
+   claims name different single services should be 0).
+2. Every former failure: gold claim `supported` with `independent_units.department=2`, rival claim `hypothesis` with the
+   "span 1 department unit(s)" reason, no `contested` in the view.
+3. Unit tests: (i) same service, different numbers → conflict opened, both contested; (ii) different services, same context
+   → no conflict, both committed, gate ranks them; (iii) contradiction follow-up routes to the holders behind both sides.
+4. Goal-only tasks (10) and the 6 passing cross_domain tasks that currently carry `contested` claims must still pass.
